            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Keeps the forearms and hands out of the torso, and on its surface where they should be touching. A real pose never puts a hand inside the body, whichever arm a sensor error has moved, so this is an absolute reference - where pose_symmetry can only compare one arm with the other.\n\nTHE BODY:\nThe torso is an oval cylinder from the pelvis to the upper spine, turned with the chest:\n'torso width' and 'torso depth' are its half-width and half-depth in cm. The forearms and hands are points along them, placed by the Shadow skeleton, with 'arm radius' of thickness. Sizes are for an average adult; set them for the dancer.\n\nWHAT COUNTS:\nIn every frame, how deep any forearm or hand point is inside the torso (a hand clear of the body counts nothing). In the frames flagged as 'contact ranges' - hands resting on the hips, say - how far each wrist is from the surface, in or out.\n\nIts loss is for t.optimize, added to the others with +, to learn shadow_mag_correct's weights.\n\nSYNTAX:\nbody_contact\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first. A tensor carrying gradients keeps them.\n\ncontact ranges:\nAn option: frames where the wrists rest on the body, like 1-827, 15428-15741. Empty checks penetration only.\n\nevery:\nAn option: if the take was thinned (t[] ::6), the step, so the ranges still name frames of the full take.\n\ntorso width, torso depth:\nOptions, in cm (13, 10): the torso's half-width and half-depth.\n\narm radius:\nAn option, in cm (4): the thickness of the forearm and hand.\n\ncontact weight:\nAn option (1): how much the contact counts against the penetration.\n\nrobust width:\nAn option, in cm (1). Misses smaller than this count by their square, larger ones only in proportion.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nOne number for t.optimize; smaller is better.\n\ninside:\nTwo numbers, left then right: the percentage of frames with that forearm or hand inside the torso.\n\ncontact gap:\nTwo numbers, left then right, in cm: each wrist's typical distance from the surface in the contact ranges; negative is inside. Sent only when there are contact ranges.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both arms hanging, swaying in and out together: as mirror images, clear of the body (0 and 0), then with both turned 15 degrees toward the dancer's right - the left arm swings in through the body for most of the take (about 71), the right one out (0).\n\nRELATED:\npose_symmetry compares the arms with each other; hang_symmetry measures the shared shift of hanging arms; shadow_mag_correct is the correction. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1350,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Keeps the forearms and hands out of the torso, and on its surface where they should be touching. A real pose never puts a hand inside the body, whichever arm a sensor error has moved, so this is an absolute reference - where pose_symmetry can only compare one arm with the other.\n\nTHE BODY:\nThe torso is an oval cylinder from the pelvis to the upper spine, turned with the chest:\n'torso width' and 'torso depth' are its half-width and half-depth in cm. The forearms and hands are points along them, placed by the Shadow skeleton, with 'arm radius' of thickness. Sizes are for an average adult; set them for the dancer.\n\nWHAT COUNTS:\nIn every frame, how deep any forearm or hand point is inside the torso (a hand clear of the body counts nothing). In the frames flagged as 'contact ranges' - hands resting on the hips, say - how far each wrist is from the surface, in or out.\n\nIts loss is for t.optimize, added to the others with +, to learn shadow_mag_correct's weights.\n\nSYNTAX:\nbody_contact\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first. A tensor carrying gradients keeps them.\n\ncontact ranges:\nAn option: frames where the wrists rest on the body, like 1-827, 15428-15741. Empty checks penetration only.\n\nevery:\nAn option: if the take was thinned (t[] ::6), the step, so the ranges still name frames of the full take.\n\ntorso width, torso depth:\nOptions, in cm (13, 10): the torso's half-width and half-depth.\n\narm radius:\nAn option, in cm (4): the thickness of the forearm and hand.\n\ncontact weight:\nAn option (1): how much the contact counts against the penetration.\n\nrobust width:\nAn option, in cm (1). Misses smaller than this count by their square, larger ones only in proportion.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nloss:\nOne number for t.optimize; smaller is better.\n\ninside:\nTwo numbers, left then right: the percentage of frames with that forearm or hand inside the torso.\n\ncontact gap:\nTwo numbers, left then right, in cm: each wrist's typical distance from the surface in the contact ranges; negative is inside. Sent only when there are contact ranges.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both arms hanging, swaying in and out together: as mirror images, clear of the body (0 and 0), then with both turned 15 degrees toward the dancer's right - the left arm swings in through the body for most of the take (about 71), the right one out (0).\n\nRELATED:\npose_symmetry compares the arms with each other; hang_symmetry measures the shared shift of hanging arms; shadow_mag_correct is the correction. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json."
        },
